window.docContents[195].resolve({"/part-3-internal/#part-3-internal":{"contents":"Why doesn't the Dedekind completeness axiom of \\mathbb{R}—“every nonempty bounded-above subset has a least upper bound”—transfer directly to prove that the set of infinitesimals \\mathbb{I} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^* has a least upper bound s \\in \\mathbb{R}^*? (Notice such an s is impossible: if s \\in \\mathbb{I} then 2s \\in \\mathbb{I} exceeds s, while if s \\notin \\mathbb{I} then s/2 is already a smaller upper bound!)\n\nPart III answers this fundamental question with razor precision: quantifier transfer over subsets of \\mathbb{R}^* applies precisely to internal sets—those assembled index-by-index from sequences of subsets (A_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} of \\mathbb{R}! Sets like \\mathbb{I}, \\mathbb{L}, \\mathbb{N}, and \\mathbb{R} are external: invisible to internal first-order comprehension, yet extraordinarily fertile as boundary markers that force internal sets to overflow and underflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lectures on the Hyperreals — Interactive Lean 4 & Spatial Edition","header":"3. Part III — Internal & External Entities","id":"/part-3-internal/#part-3-internal"}});